# Virtio entropy and portable TCP sequence source (G)

G is implemented in `baremetal/runtime/src/virtio/rng.rs`. It provides a bounded,
polling entropy service and a callback for `TcpPool::with_sequence_source`. The
QEMU runner supplies host entropy through virtio-rng. No global entropy singleton,
Linux driver port, dependency, timestamp seed or deterministic fallback was added.

The runtime remains single-core, MMU/caches off and no IOMMU. The source depends
on the trusted host RNG backend; these tests establish transport, ownership and
integration behavior, not the statistical or cryptographic quality of a device.

## Shared transport and discovery

`virtio/mmio.rs` now has one lifecycle implementation, `Transport<R, T, QUEUES>`,
with typed constructors:

| Transport | Device ID | Queues | Required / accepted features |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `NetTransport<R, T>` | 1 | RX 0, TX 1 | VERSION_1 + MAC, optionally STATUS |
| `RngTransport<R, T>` | 4 | request 0 | VERSION_1 only |

Network methods and configuration access remain on the network specialization.
Entropy does not read MAC, link status or device configuration fields. Each device
retains its own register owner, queue storage and reset state. Sharing the
allocator does not share reset authority. The fixed QEMU slot probe now selects
by device kind, so either device may exist without the other.

Both transports use the same feature negotiation, split-ring publication,
completion validation, notification ordering and reset/quarantine behavior from
B/C. There is no second copy of those algorithms. Existing network tests still
exercise the shared implementation.

The protocol basis is [Virtio 1.2, section 5.4](https://docs.oasis-open.org/virtio/virtio/v1.2/virtio-v1.2.html):
entropy requests are writable buffers, and completion length determines how many
bytes may be consumed. Short nonempty responses are valid. This implementation
uses one direct writable descriptor and does not negotiate device-specific or
optional ring features.

## Ownership and fixed bounds

The default service uses:

- One descriptor in one split queue, backed by one 4 KiB DMA page.
- One exclusively owned 4 KiB request region, exposing 256 bytes per request.
- A 1024-byte CPU-only ring buffer for completed entropy, plus small heap metadata.

Request and cache sizes are configurable up to 4096 bytes. Cache capacity must
be at least the request size. Supporting a TCP sequence source additionally
requires room for four bytes. General one-byte entropy consumers can use a
smaller cache.

The request region has exactly one owner: either the driver's idle `spare` slot
or the transport's in-flight entry. No CPU slice is created while that region is
owned by the device. Valid completion acquires DMA writes before returning the
region, then copies only the reported bytes into the CPU cache. That small copy
lets consumers read completed entropy while the device fills the same DMA region
again. Completed data is never served directly from an active DMA buffer.

The cache invariant is `cached bytes + reserved request bytes <= capacity`.
Before posting, the service reserves space for a full response. Short replies
release the unused reservation; they do not expose old bytes from a previous
request. At most one request is in flight. There is no per-request allocation.

`poll()` processes at most one completion and posts at most one new request.
Even one-byte responses cannot create an unbounded refill loop. A full cache
stops submissions until enough capacity is free for another whole request.
Polling still checks device status while the cache is full. `pending()`,
`available()`, `stats()` and `fault()` expose progress without exposing DMA memory.

This owned-region path uses the caches-off DMA contract directly. No payload
cache maintenance is needed in that domain; the shared ring supplies barriers.
A future cache-enabled platform must add correct synchronization before using
this implementation there.

## Consumption, errors and shutdown

`try_fill(out)` is all-or-nothing:

| Situation | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Enough completed bytes | Fill `out`, consume each byte once |
| Cache temporarily short | `WouldBlock`; destination and cache unchanged |
| Request exceeds cache capacity | `InvalidInput`; destination unchanged |
| Service stopped | `NotConnected`; destination unchanged |
| Device/protocol fault | Error; no cached entropy is returned |

The consumer path performs no MMIO, allocation, refill or wait. A busy shared
borrow also returns `WouldBlock`. Refill is the main loop's responsibility.
Consumed cache slots are cleared, and fault/shutdown discards cached bytes. This
is ordinary memory clearing, not a guarantee of secure erasure of all copies.

A zero-length completion violates the entropy protocol and latches a fault.
Oversized lengths or corrupt ring metadata use the common transport fault path;
the suspicious DMA owner remains retained. Detection of a device fault discards
the CPU cache and stops further requests. There is no substitute entropy source.
A missing completion simply leaves the request in flight; elapsed time does not
authorize reuse.

`VirtioRng` handles clone an `Rc<RefCell<...>>`, not the register/queue owner. This
matches the single-threaded runtime and keeps it `!Send`/`!Sync`. A TCP callback
retains the service, so dropping the main-loop handle alone does not release DMA
memory while a callback survives. Keep a handle for polling and explicit shutdown.

`shutdown()` is terminal for every clone/callback. It discards cached bytes,
requests device reset and releases DMA storage only after confirmation. A failed
reset can be retried and retains the outstanding request. Successful cancellation
is counted once. Last-owner Drop performs the same transport reset, quarantining
the active ring and request on failure. An idle request region is already
quiescent and can be freed independently. For reinitialization, confirm shutdown
and drop all old handles/callbacks before opening the device again.

## TCP integration and backpressure

After the service has at least four bytes available, wire it into the existing
portable API:

```rust
let source = entropy.tcp_sequence_source()?;
let mut tcp = TcpPool::with_sequence_source(device, tcp_config, source)?;
```

The source factory checks readiness without consuming bytes. It returns
`WouldBlock` if entropy has not arrived, or `InvalidInput` if the configured cache
can never hold four bytes. Propagating that result prevents construction of the
TCP pool before entropy is available. The portable `TcpPool::new` still returns
`Unsupported` because portable builds have no implicit OS entropy provider.

Each callback invocation consumes four fresh cached bytes and converts them to
a `u32`. The byte-order convention is explicit little endian; all values,
including zero, are valid. The callback neither polls the device nor seeds a
local PRNG. Readiness is not a reservation: another consumer can drain the cache
between factory creation and a TCP open, so later callback errors remain possible.

Main-loop integration is explicit: poll entropy with bounded work before network
processing, and handle entropy errors according to application policy. An active
`connect` that encounters `WouldBlock` creates no connection and consumes no ID;
it can be retried after refill. During a passive open, `TcpPool::poll` can return
`WouldBlock`, recycles the received SYN and creates no connection. After refill,
a retransmitted SYN can be processed normally. Existing established connections
do not need a fresh sequence number for every data packet. Missing entropy does
not require shutting down UDP.

The guest boot check obtains 4096 bytes over repeated refills, constructs a TCP
pool using the real entropy callback, and checks that an active open consumes
four bytes and reaches `SynSent`. That construction check uses `LoopbackDevice`;
it does **not** claim a TCP handshake or stream exchange with the host. A synthetic
peer test checks a real encoded SYN-ACK against known supplied entropy, including
cache exhaustion and retry. End-to-end host TCP traffic is a separate harness
extension.

## Validation and commands

```sh
python3 scripts/qemu-virt-net.py                  # entropy + UDP, serial shown
python3 scripts/qemu-virt-net.py --smoke
python3 scripts/qemu-virt-net.py --smoke --release
cargo test --locked --manifest-path baremetal/Cargo.toml -p async-net-baremetal-runtime
cargo test --locked -p async-net-stack-core
cargo fmt --all -- --check
cargo fmt --manifest-path baremetal/Cargo.toml --all -- --check
```

The runtime suite has 56 tests: the existing 45 plus one entropy transport test
and ten RNG/TCP tests. The new tests cover feature/queue isolation, short reads,
cache wrap/capacity, unchanged output on error, absent/legacy devices, invalid
configuration, setup allocation failure, no completion, zero/oversized responses,
fault after publication, reset retry/quarantine, token exhaustion, shared-source
lifetime, and active/passive TCP entropy exhaustion/recovery. Synthetic devices
write the actual ring and published DMA ranges; deterministic bytes stay in tests.

The runner uses explicit QEMU `rng-random` configuration with `/dev/urandom`,
attached as `virtio-rng-device`. Entropy bytes are not printed. Full debug/release
smoke covers:

1. Modern NIC and RNG: repeated entropy refill, TCP source integration, 256 UDP
   echoes across NIC reset, and continued RNG operation after each NIC reset.
2. Rate-limited RNG (`max-bytes=3,period=1`): real short responses, checked by a
   positive short-read counter, with the same entropy and UDP scenarios.
3. No RNG: explicit TCP-unavailable diagnostic, with all UDP echoes still passing.
4. No NIC: RNG discovery, operation and reset succeed independently.
5. Legacy transports: unsupported-device diagnostics without modern setup.
6. Heap allocation failure: the existing bounded panic/halt check.

After these scenarios, RNG reset/reinitialization is checked and every DMA page
must return. The boot-only readiness helper waits at most five seconds per
readiness operation; normal packet processing never uses that helper. Host smoke
retains its overall timeout and kills failed guests.

Root `cargo fmt` covers the host and portable workspace. The guest's independent
`[workspace]` means it needs the second formatting command above. Both checks now
pass; the previously reported guest formatting differences have been fixed.

## Remaining work and division

A–D and G now work for this QEMU polling target. The next runtime package is
**F: interrupts and race-free wakeups**. E remains useful parallel human work;
physical hardware remains H.

- **F / runtime:** choose and document the QEMU GIC version and IRQ routing;
  implement resumable vectors with full required register preservation; bring up
  the timer interrupt; route NIC and RNG notifications to pending-work flags.
  Keep `Rc` pools, entropy consumption and transport mutation in the main loop.
  Define queue notification enabling and a race-free check/enable/sleep sequence;
  current split queues request suppressed interrupts. Run the same UDP/entropy
  scenarios in polling and IRQ modes, including idle wakeups and reset.
- **E / peer and integration:** add host TCP handshake/stream tests using this
  source, concurrent TCP/UDP dispatch through the coordinator, failure/capture
  artifacts, starvation/late-RNG recovery under connections, and packet fault
  injection. The current TCP boot check verifies source use, not host delivery.
- **H / platform:** device-tree discovery and reservations, MMU attributes,
  coherent/noncoherent DMA cache maintenance, physical board/NIC selection and
  hardware stress. Select a trusted entropy source for that board; virtio-rng
  assumes a hypervisor-provided device and is not a physical TRNG driver.

See the [broader work plan](virtio-net-plan.md) and the
[network adapter design](virtio-net-adapter.md) for the other integration boundaries.

